A||||2||jmyokley||86 Headlight problem
Z||000000||jmyokley||05-10-99||02:15 PM||||Fiero Friends,<p>I have tried to e-mail many different people on the world wide Fiero web and<br>get no feedback.<p>I have a 86 GT and have what I consider a weird headlight problem. I have<br>read everything I can find on the web (and manuals) and nothing answers my problem. I have gone through all the troubleshooting I can think of.<p>My headlights work as designed, i.e....they raise and lower on command, but with<br>just the headlights up (engine not on) both actuator motors heat up (warm<br>and almost hot to the touch) and both make a clicking noise...if you touch<br>the actuator motor you can feel the motor "jump" when you hear the clicking<br>noise. With the headlights down the right actuator motor still stays hot and<br>clicks but the left actuator motor cools down and quits making the clicking<br>noise.<p>Is it normal for the actuator motors to heat up? (I don't think so).<br>Is it normal for the actuator motors to click every few seconds? (I don't think so).<br>Could I have a wiring problem or a bad motor brush/switch assembly, or bad relay?<br>If not do you have an idea of what could be the problem. Other than this the<br>system works fine.<p>Any help would be appreciated.<p><br>John Yokley<br>281-233-5713<br>jyokley@smith-intl.com
Z||000001||PaUL||05-10-99||03:55 PM||||First you need to know is which headlight motors you have, there were 2 designs. Since your fiero is an 86, it probably has the older type with mechanical reed switches inside. These turn the motors off when fully open or closed, if they arent working the motors will continue to get current even when fully open or closed. There is also a self resetting circuit breaker that cuts off the power when too much current is drawn. This is the clicking sound you hear. Its a small glass "thing". <p>You need to open the motor up by drilling out the rivets that hold the plastic case together. Then check too see why the reed switches arent working. They work by tension, when the tightness increases they are supposed to cut the power.<p>An alternative simpler fix would be to rig a switch to cut the power manually. You should never bypass the glass circuit breaker by shorting it. I did this once and the reed switches failed, it totally fried the motor. The plastic case got melted. 
Z||000002||Carrolles||05-11-99||10:45 AM||||You don't have to drill out the rivets and crack the case to look at the reed switches.  There is a rectangular cover plate with either 3 or 4 screws that gives access to the reed switches and brushes.  <br>Hope this helps,<br>Carroll
